# Both editions have additional written prompts, with distinct question wording.
W=[
(2,'Network roles','Describe how a formant recording becomes input to the five-word classifier. Include the roles of hidden units and connection weights.','Identify the inputs, hidden-layer sizes, and output labels in the supplied classifier.', 'F1 and F2 supply the two inputs. Three hidden layers contain three, two, and four units. Weighted connections combine values across layers before five output positions represent done, dune, dean, Don, and Dan.', ['Name the two formants and five word categories.','Distinguish the three hidden layers from the input and output positions.']),
(2,'Signed contributions','Two connections receive the same positive input but have weights with opposite signs. Explain their contributions to a receiving weighted sum.','For a positive incoming value, how do positive and negative connection weights differ?', 'Multiplication by a positive weight gives a positive contribution, while multiplication by a negative weight gives a negative contribution. The receiver adds contributions from all its inputs, so one signed contribution does not determine the full value by itself.', ['Identify the sign change in multiplication.','Keep a connection contribution distinct from the full combined result.']),
(3,'Trace the forward pass','A network has fixed weights and receives a new F1–F2 pair. Explain why hidden values must be computed in layer order.','Why can the second hidden layer not be evaluated before the first hidden-layer values are available?', 'Each receiving layer uses the outputs of the layer immediately before it. The formants determine first-layer weighted sums, those determine the next layer, and the sequence continues until final scores and probabilities are available.', ['Identify the previous layer as the immediate input.','Describe the forward dependency without treating weights as updated during the pass.']),
(3,'Inner-product meaning','Explain the operation that gives the top hidden neuron −0.40 from the formants 320 and 2000. Explain what that result represents.','What is an inner product in this formant network?', 'Each formant is multiplied by its connection weight and the two products are added. The resulting −0.40 is that hidden neuron’s linear activation, not an output probability or a statement that the neuron has been removed.', ['Name multiplication and addition.','Identify the resulting quantity as a hidden activation.']),
(3,'Logits and probabilities','The final weighted sums include negative values, but all five displayed probabilities are positive. Explain how these can both be correct.','Distinguish a logit from a softmax probability.', 'A logit is a final score before normalization and may be negative. Softmax exponentiates the five scores and divides by their shared total. This produces positive probabilities that sum to one.', ['Distinguish unnormalized scores from probabilities.','Explain the shared exponential normalization.']),
(6,'Prediction and desired output','The network favors done, but the target has its 1 in the dean position. Explain what the two representations say about this recording.','What do the computed output and desired output each represent?', 'The computed output is the network’s current probability distribution, with done highest. The desired output is the supplied correct label, dean. The mismatch is an error to learn from, not a reason to relabel the recording as done.', ['Identify the current favored prediction.','Identify the target independently of that prediction.']),
(6,'Training direction','The true word is dean. Explain how increasing or decreasing its assigned probability changes cross-entropy loss.','Which change in dean’s probability reduces the loss for a dean target?', 'Increasing the probability of dean reduces its cross-entropy loss. Decreasing that probability increases the loss. The target remains dean while the network parameters are adjusted.', ['State the correct direction.','Keep the true target fixed.']),
(6,'Gradient update','A weight has a negative loss gradient. Describe its update under the positive-learning-rate subtraction rule and explain why the direction is appropriate.','Does SGD increase or decrease a weight with a negative loss gradient?', 'It increases the weight because subtracting a negative scaled gradient adds to the old value. A negative local gradient means a small increase in that weight lowers the loss for the current calculation.', ['State increase.','Relate the subtraction rule to the direction of loss change.']),
(6,'Fixed activations during backward calculation','Weights are revealed in updated form from the output side backward, while neuron values stay unchanged. Explain why this is not four independent forward-and-update cycles.','Why are the stored forward values retained throughout the backward pass?', 'The gradients for all layers belong to the same original forward computation. They use its stored activations and original weights. The reveal sequence displays one simultaneous update, and updated predictions require a separate new forward pass.', ['Link all gradients to one stored computation.','Distinguish reveal order from recomputing after each visible update.']),
(6,'Input learning rate','The numerical example gives input-layer weights a smaller learning rate than the later layers. Explain the source-supported reason.','Why does the hand-worked network use a smaller input-layer learning rate?', 'The raw F1 and F2 values are in hertz and much larger than the later hidden values. Their scale enters the input-weight gradients, so the supplied example uses a smaller learning rate for those connections.', ['Identify raw formant scale.','Connect the different update scale to the input-weight gradients.']),
(7,'Marked output connection','The pink-marked connection carries a positive hidden value into done, while dean is correct. Explain the effect of a small increase in that weight on the loss.','Why does increasing the marked connection to done raise the dean-target loss?', 'The positive source value makes done’s score rise when that connection weight rises. Softmax allocates one total across candidates, so dean’s probability falls as the wrong candidate gains. Cross-entropy for the dean target therefore increases.', ['Trace the positive contribution to done.','Connect shared probabilities to the loss on dean.']),
(7,'Marked early connection','The blue F1 weight is positive. Explain why that sign alone cannot tell whether increasing it improves the final classification.','What extra information is required to judge an early weight’s effect on final error?', 'Its effect continues through later weighted connections, including paths with different signs, and is evaluated against the output target. Backpropagation combines these downstream influences. The supplied network has a positive gradient for this weight, but that direction is not guaranteed by its own positive sign.', ['Mention downstream weighted paths.','Avoid inferring error direction from one connection’s sign alone.']),
(8,'Last versus first weights','Explain why attributing error to W2 requires information about later weights, while the W5 calculation is closer to the output comparison.','Why is the final weight layer easier to relate directly to output error than the first?', 'W5 directly forms the final scores from the last hidden activations. W2 changes first-layer hidden activity, whose effects continue through the later layers. Its contribution to loss therefore includes those downstream transformations.', ['Identify the direct final-layer relationship.','Describe the indirect early-layer dependency.']),
(8,'Dynamic-programming connection','Explain what is reused when the course connects backpropagation with Bellman–Viterbi dynamic programming.','How does reusing downstream error calculations help with earlier weights?', 'The effects of later layers on error are computed first. Earlier-layer calculations combine their local effects with those retained downstream results rather than recalculating all later effects separately for every connection.', ['Identify retained downstream results.','Explain their reuse for earlier dependencies.']),
(9,'Training-matrix layout','Describe the meanings of rows and columns in the 110 × 2 input matrix and 110 × 5 target matrix.','What do the matching 110 rows mean, and why do the column counts differ?', 'Both matrices refer to the same 110 recordings. The input has F1 and F2 columns, while the target has one column per word candidate with a 1 at the correct category. Different column counts describe different roles for the same examples.', ['Match rows to recordings.','Distinguish two measurements from five category positions.']),
(9,'Paired targets','The third row contains F1 = 320 and F2 = 2000. State its target category and explain why input and target rows must remain aligned.','What target belongs with the illustrated 320, 2000 input row?', 'Its one-hot target identifies dean. Row alignment preserves the recording–label pairing. Reordering only one matrix can associate the formants of one recording with another recording’s word.', ['Identify dean.','Explain the pairing requirement.']),
(10,'Training calls','Explain the distinct purposes of zero_grad, backward, and step in the one-step program.','Which call clears gradients, which computes them, and which updates parameters?', 'zero_grad clears previously stored gradients. backward computes the parameter gradients from the loss. step applies the SGD update to the parameters. These operations keep gradient storage, derivative calculation, and parameter change distinct.', ['Assign the three calls correctly.','Do not equate clearing gradients with clearing weights.']),
(10,'Batch training','The code sends all 110 recordings through net(X), computes one loss, and calls step once. Explain the number of updates and the role of the batch.','Does the shown program perform one or 110 optimizer updates?', 'It performs one update using the loss over the full 110-row batch. Multiple examples contribute to that gradient calculation, but there is no repeated optimizer call per row and no outer training loop.', ['State one full-batch update.','Distinguish examples in a batch from the number of optimizer steps.']),
(10,'Architecture versus parameters','Two networks both use 2 → 3 → 2 → 4 → 5 layers. Explain why one may not reproduce the diagram’s exact probabilities.','What must match besides layer sizes to reproduce the displayed numerical network?', 'The parameter values and bias choices must match. The illustration has particular hand-selected weights and no biases, whereas the short PyTorch declarations initialize default parameters and bias terms.', ['Mention parameter values.','Mention the different bias choices.']),
(11,'Sigmoid interpretation','Explain why an inner product of −4 gives a near-zero activation and an inner product of 4 gives a near-one activation.','Describe the relationship between the sign of a large inner product and sigmoid output.', 'Sigmoid approaches zero for large negative inputs and one for large positive inputs. Its displayed values are about 0.0180 and 0.9820. It is a continuous bounded transformation, not an exact binary cutoff.', ['Match negative to near zero and positive to near one.','Retain the graded rather than exact on/off behavior.']),
(11,'Sigmoid and perceived differences','Use the flat ends and steep middle of sigmoid to explain how different input differences can be compressed or emphasized.','How can distinct inputs give similar activations near the sigmoid’s ends?', 'Near a flat end, the output changes little as the input changes, so distinct inputs can yield similar activations. Near the steep middle, relatively small input changes can produce larger activation changes.', ['Describe compression near a flat end.','Contrast the more sensitive central region.']),
(12,'Sequential sigmoid changes','The first hidden value becomes 0.401 instead of −0.400. Explain why applying sigmoid to all the old stored hidden values separately would not reproduce the supplied sequence.','Why must downstream weighted sums be recomputed after an earlier sigmoid is introduced?', 'Later neurons receive the changed early output. Their new weighted sums must therefore be calculated from those new inputs before sigmoid is applied at their positions. The demonstration builds the altered network in layer order.', ['Identify changed incoming values for later neurons.','Distinguish recomputation from transforming an unrelated stored linear result.']),
(12,'Activation change versus learning','The network changes its largest response from done to Dan as hidden sigmoids are added. Explain what changes, what stays fixed, and whether this establishes successful training.','Does the sigmoid demonstration update weights or change the hidden activation rule?', 'The hidden activation rule changes while formants and weights stay fixed. Downstream computations and final probabilities change. Dan becomes the largest response, but dean remains the target, so this is not evidence that a weight-training step has learned the correct label.', ['Keep inputs and weights fixed.','Distinguish changed response from correct learned classification.']),
(13,'Placing sigmoid','Describe where nn.Sigmoid is inserted and why the input size of the next linear layer stays the same.','Does inserting sigmoid change the number of hidden values passed to the next layer?', 'Sigmoid follows each of the first three linear layers and transforms each value without changing the number of values. A three-unit hidden layer still sends three values to the next linear layer.', ['Locate sigmoid after hidden linear layers.','State that dimensionality is preserved.']),
(13,'Final output and loss','Explain why the sigmoid version leaves the final Linear(4, 5) without a declared sigmoid before CrossEntropyLoss.','Distinguish the hidden sigmoid operation from the final cross-entropy input.', 'Sigmoid transforms the hidden activations. The final linear layer supplies five logits, and cross-entropy performs the softmax-related loss calculation internally. Independent hidden activation and final class comparison serve different roles.', ['Identify final values as logits.','Distinguish hidden sigmoid from loss normalization.'])
]

def build():
 for version in [1,2]:
  rng=random.Random(285150+version);qs=[];positions=[i%4 for i in range(len(M))];rng.shuffle(positions)
  for i,(page,topic,p1,p2,correct,wrong)in enumerate(M,1):
   options=[{'text':t,'feedback':f}for t,f in wrong];rng.shuffle(options);options.insert(positions[i-1],{'text':correct,'feedback':correct})
   answer_id=None
   for label,opt in zip('abcd',options):
    opt['id']=label
    if opt['text']==correct:answer_id=label
   qs.append({'id':f'{"v2-l15" if version==2 else "ling285-l15"}-mcq-{i:03}','type':'mcq','moduleId':'module2','lectureId':'lecture15','prompt':p1 if version==1 else p2,'answer':correct,'rubric':['Identify the source-supported relationship.', 'Distinguish it from the stated alternative explanations.'],'sources':[{'collection':'lecture15','page':page}],'topic':topic,'pattern':'interpretation' if i in [1,3,4,6,7,16,20,22,25]else 'distinction' if i in [2,10,15,18,19,21]else 'prediction','examKeyRefs':[],'examReviewRefs':[],'options':options,'correctOption':answer_id})
  for i,(page,topic,p1,p2,answer,rubric)in enumerate(W,1):
   qs.append({'id':f'{"v2-l15" if version==2 else "ling285-l15"}-written-{i:03}','type':'written','moduleId':'module2','lectureId':'lecture15','prompt':p1 if version==1 else p2,'answer':answer,'rubric':rubric,'sources':[{'collection':'lecture15','page':page}],'topic':topic,'pattern':'cause-effect' if i in [3,5,7,10,11,12,13,14,21,22]else 'distinction' if i in [6,9,17,18,19,23,24,25]else 'interpretation','examKeyRefs':[],'examReviewRefs':[]})
  name='practice-v2-lecture15.json'if version==2 else'module2-lecture15-practice.json'
  (A/name).write_text(json.dumps({'lectureId':'lecture15','questions':qs},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
if __name__=='__main__':build()
